    {
      "filename": "A Collection of Ethiopian Liturgical Drums (3424574812).jpg",
      "title": "File:A Collection of Ethiopian Liturgical Drums (3424574812).jpg",
      "url": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/49/A_Collection_of_Ethiopian_Liturgical_Drums_%283424574812%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original",
      "descriptionUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_Collection_of_Ethiopian_Liturgical_Drums_(3424574812).jpg",
      "width": 2756,
      "height": 1677,
      "mime": "image/jpeg",
      "license": "CC BY 2.0",
      "licenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0",
      "artist": "A. Davey from Where I Live Now: Pacific Northwest",
      "description": "I saw a drum or two on the floor at almost every Ethiopian Orthodox church I visited, but the Church at the Monastery of Na’akuto La’ab near Lalibela had the largest and most diverse collection.\nI understand the term for these drums is kebero.  According to the source of all knowledge, Wikipedia:\n\"A kebero is a double-headed, conical hand drum used in the traditional music of Eritrea and Ethiopia. A piece of animal hide is stretched over each end, thus forming a membranophone.\" \n\"A large version of the instrument is used in Orthodox Christian liturgical music, while smaller versions are used in secular celebrations.\"\nen.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kebero\nBased on that description, I'm confident the three drums in the foreground are keberos.\nThe two other drums, one of which is barely visible, are more tub-shaped than conical, so I don't know whether they, too, would be considered keberos.\nInterestingly, the stick used to beat the drum on the far left is visible on the drum's surface.  \nNot having heard drums of this type being played while I was in Ethiopia, I can't comment on the sound.  However, I'm fascinated by two aspects of these musical instruments.\nThe first are the printed fabrics with which the drums are covered.  Two of the three conical drums in this photo are unusual in that their fabric is in remarkably good condition.   Over time and with use, the fabric has usually become more tattered.\nObservant viewers may recognize the red fabric with a floral pattern from other  photos, including a photo of one of the manuscripts the priest showed us during our visit to this monastery.  That photo appears below for your reference.\nThe second thing I love about the kebero is the web of leather or sinew that keeps the heads taut and may also contribute to the drum's structural integrity.  \n\nI'm speculating here, but in some cases the web of sinew looks so taut that it might have been applied wet and allowed to dry in situ, where it would stretch as it dried.",
      "dateCreated": "2007-11-02 02:48",
      "categories": [
        "CC-BY-2.0",
        "Christianity in Ethiopia",
        "Files created by A.Davey",
        "Flickr images reviewed by File Upload Bot (Magnus Manske)",
        "Kebero",
